THE RUNTIME REPAIRS OF THE FORK, AS PATCHES FOR UPSTREAM

The fork changes its copy of the tree-sitter runtime in vendor/tree-sitter. This directory holds one
patch file for each repair that upstream can accept. The 32-bit parse tables of the fork (ABI 1015)
are not here, because upstream does not want a second ABI.

Each patch applies to the upstream tag v0.27.0 of https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter, after
the patches with a smaller number. The full series applies with `git apply` and no fuzz, and the
result compiles with `-Wall -Wextra` and no warning:

    git archive v0.27.0 | tar -x -C /tmp/ts
    cd /tmp/ts && for p in upstream-patches/*.patch; do git apply "$p"; done

Each patch file gives the fork commit, the measurement from that commit, and the risk for upstream.

BEFORE A SUBMISSION

The comments of the fork hold the marker "(tree-sitter-cpp fork)". Remove the marker from each
comment before you send a patch upstream.

THE PATCHES

06-reduce-each-added-version.patch is the strongest candidate. Upstream v0.27.0 still has the
defect at lib/src/parser.c:1212: the loop of `ts_parser__do_all_potential_reductions` goes to
`version_count` after the starting version, so it does not do the reductions of the versions that
the starting version added. A removal of a version also moves the versions after it, and the loop
then skips one. The patch repairs the two errors. Risk: low.

The other patches, in the order of the series:

01-summary-depth.patch
    Raise the depth of the stack summary of error recovery from 16 to 256. A better form for
    upstream puts `#ifndef` around each of the five limits of lib/src/parser.c and lib/src/stack.c,
    so that a build flag sets them. Risk: low.

02-summary-for-version-with-none.patch
    Record a summary for a version in the error state that has none. Without a summary, the version
    skips each token until the end of the input. Risk: low.

03-unmatched-bracket-cost.patch
    Give a cost to each right bracket that has no left bracket in an ERROR node. The patch reads the
    names of the token symbols to find the brackets, and it adds 8 bytes to `SubtreeHeapData`.
    An alternative gives the bracket symbols in the language. Risk: HIGH.

04-recover-to-three-states.patch
    Recover to three previous states for one lookahead token, and not only to the nearest one. The
    value 3 comes from a measurement on 329,387 C++ files. Risk: medium.

05-breakdown-lookahead-with-no-action.patch
    Read a reused lookahead node with no action as its first leaf. An incremental parse then gives
    the tree of a fresh parse in more inputs. Risk: low.

07-saturate-error-costs.patch
    Stop the sums of the error costs at the maximum of 32 bits. A sum in 32 bits can become smaller
    than one of its parts, and a stack version then gets a cost that is too small. Risk: low.

08-missing-braces-at-end-of-input.patch
    Close each open block with a MISSING `}` at the end of the input. The patch reads the names of
    the token symbols to find `}` and `;`. An alternative gives the symbols of the missing tokens in
    the language. Risk: HIGH.

09-missing-semicolon-after-class.patch
    Close a class body at the end of the input with a MISSING `;`. The patch follows patch 08 and
    has the same risk. Risk: HIGH.

10-incremental-reuse.patch
    Give an incremental parse the tree of a fresh parse. The patch has four parts that upstream can
    accept one at a time: a node with an error cost is not reused as one subtree, a node with a
    fragile child becomes fragile, a reused node that the parser shifts goes onto the stack as the
    subtrees of its right spine, and a reused node with more than one stack version breaks down to
    its first leaf. Risk: medium, because of the size.

11-merge-finished-version.patch
    Merge a stack version with an equivalent version before it, as soon as the version loop
    completes the work of that version. Without the merge, the list holds more versions than the
    mid-step limit of `ts_parser__reduce`, and that limit removes a reduction by the index of its
    version. The reading of a deep ambiguity then depends on the state numbers, with no ERROR node.
    The patch text says why a removal at the same place is not safe, because an upstream reader will
    otherwise propose one. Risk: medium.

12-column-anchor.patch
    Read the line of a column one time for each line, and not for each token. `ts_lexer__get_column`
    reads the line again from its start at each call, and an external scanner that reads the column
    one time for each token of a line then takes a time that grows with the square of the length of
    the line. A file of 50,000 statements `x = a % b;` on one line took 38.9 s, and the same
    statements on 50,000 lines took 0.42 s. Risk: low.

WHAT UPSTREAM ALREADY HAS

Upstream v0.27.0 has its own non-recursive S-expression writer, and it replaces the writer of the
fork. Upstream v0.26.13 has the repair of the wide error nodes (#5801 and #5855) and the repair of
the error costs through hidden error nodes. The patches of this directory sit on top of those.
